Q: How to work around Linux&#39;s lack of mandatory file locking? A: <p>If you need to be sure that the file is opened by only one application at a time, you can use <code>flock()</code> function.</p>
<pre><code>int fd = open(&quot;somefile&quot;, O_RDWR | O_CLOEXEC);

if (flock(fd, LOCK_EX | LOCK_NB) != 0 ) {
   fprintf(stderr, &quot;Unable to lock; %m&quot;);
   exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
}
// at this moment you know that file is opened by you exclusively 
// do whatever you need

flock(fd, LOCK_UN);
// lock is removed, but file is still opened
// now some other app can access it and you still have access too
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>LOCK_NB</code> is useful here if you want to fail and report an error, in case the file is opened by someone else. If you use <code>flock()</code> with just <code>LOCK_EX</code> and some other app is using that file - then <code>flock()</code> will freeze until the file is released.</p>

Q: How to simulate real world dimensions in css? A: <p>Use css height and width.</p>

<p>The height and width can be set to auto (this is default. Means that the browser calculates the height and width), or be specified in length values, like px, cm, etc., or in percent (%) of the containing block.</p>

<p>So if for example you want to show 35cm width and 50cm height element you can set it with css:</p>

<pre><code>#example {
  height: 50cm;
  width: 35cm;
}
</code></pre>

<p>Good luck!</p>

